finding: 1. Cost qualification is incomplete: stage 2's software trilinear sampling expands each AO depth sample into eight textureLoad operations (ssao-shaders.ts:118-159), instead of Bevy ssao.wesl:97's single textureSampleLevel with its linear sampler. Low therefore issues 32 horizon depth loads per pixel and High 144, besides centre/edge/normal/noise work. The table faithfully measures this implementation, but does not establish that its 1.30/4.54 ms evaluation cost is an unavoidable cost of the port. WebGPU core permits a filterable, render-attachment r16float mip chain; render-pass preprocess/downsample preserves the default feature floor. Bevy prefers R16Float where storage is supported. A lower-code-churn A/B could instead use core rgba16float storage for depth and hardware filtering. Qualify one representative row against a hardware-filtered core path, with visual/precision checks, before treating this cost as settled. No speedup ratio has been independently measured; eight instructions versus one sampling instruction is not eight times the physical fetches or runtime. 2. Initial SSAO opt-in still compiles normal-prepass pipelines after the loading-screen drain. The commit explicitly records standard-prepass-StandardMaterial-0-4x and VertexMaterial-0-4x warnings. CONTRIBUTING requires initial pipelines in initialize/warm/precompile; preparePipelines gates normal preparation on Views, and must cover cameras/requests present at build. This is a stage 1/2 integration defect, not acceptable on-demand specialization for an initially configured camera. The measurement discloses/excludes the hitch correctly but cannot waive the shipped first-frame defect. 3. The default cost rerun hardcodes 120 s Ultra soak and 24 x (10 s warm + 20 s sampling), at least 14 minutes before setup. Keep this qualifying table as historical sustained-load evidence, but provide the fog-shaped default for iteration: one representative scene, 30 warm frames and a 60-frame median, with explicit full qualification mode if retained. Repeatability of the long run does not justify imposing it on every future A/B. Timing inspection otherwise passes: all seven dispatches have individual GPU timestamps, and world.step through awaited frameFence includes prepasses, SSAO, PBR and submission synchronization in Off and On. GPU span sums are not claimed as full frame cost. Warm-up also deliberately conditions sustained device load, not merely compilation. Tests independently run: ssao.test.ts and cost-report.test.ts, 4 pass/63 assertions. No source edits, full GPU rerun or alternative-path A/B performed.
